The destination buffer `ipaddr` is declared on the stack as `char ipaddr[INET6_ADDRSTRLEN + 1]` (47 bytes). However, `len` is derived directly from the unconstrained input length `str_len` minus the address prefix offset. If the port string in an input passed to `net_ipaddr_parse()` / `getaddrinfo()` / DNS resolution contains more than 46 bytes, `len` will exceed the size of `ipaddr`. `memcpy(ipaddr, ptr, len)` copies up to `len` bytes into `ipaddr`, causing a stack-based buffer overflow. Furthermore, `ipaddr[len] = '\0'` performs an out-of-bounds null-byte write on the stack.

