In `parse_recv_state()` in `subsys/bluetooth/audio/bap_broadcast_assistant.c`, the number of subgroups is parsed directly from incoming Bluetooth GATT receive state data:

```c
recv_state->num_subgroups = net_buf_simple_pull_u8(&buf);
for (int i = 0; i < recv_state->num_subgroups; i++) {
    struct bt_bap_bass_subgroup *subgroup = &recv_state->subgroups[i];
    ...
    metadata = net_buf_simple_pull_mem(&buf, subgroup->metadata_len);
    (void)memcpy(subgroup->metadata, metadata, subgroup->metadata_len);
}
```

The `recv_state->subgroups` array in `struct bt_bap_scan_delegator_recv_state` is statically sized to `CONFIG_BT_BAP_BASS_MAX_SUBGROUPS` elements. However, the code does not validate that `recv_state->num_subgroups` is less than or equal to `ARRAY_SIZE(recv_state->subgroups)` (or `CONFIG_BT_BAP_BASS_MAX_SUBGROUPS`). A malicious or compromised Bluetooth peer sending a crafted BASS receive state characteristic value with `num_subgroups` greater than `CONFIG_BT_BAP_BASS_MAX_SUBGROUPS` will cause `subgroup` to point past the end of the `subgroups` array, leading to an out-of-bounds write / buffer overflow during `memcpy(subgroup->metadata, metadata, subgroup->metadata_len)` (as well as writing `subgroup->bis_sync` and `subgroup->metadata_len`).

Data flow:
- Source: Untrusted GATT read response or notification payload (`data`, `length`) received over Bluetooth LE.
- Validation: Missing check on `recv_state->num_subgroups <= ARRAY_SIZE(recv_state->subgroups)`.
- Sink: `memcpy(subgroup->metadata, metadata, subgroup->metadata_len)` writing out of bounds of `recv_state->subgroups`.

Conceptual Exploit Payload:
A malicious BASS server responds to a read or sends a notification on the Broadcast Audio Scan Service (BASS) Receive State characteristic with:
`[src_id: 0x01][addr_type: 0x00][addr: 6 bytes][adv_sid: 0x00][broadcast_id: 3 bytes][pa_sync_state: 0x00][encrypt_state: 0x00][num_subgroups: 0xFF]` followed by repeated subgroup structures `[bis_sync: 4 bytes][metadata_len: 0x04][metadata: 4 bytes]`.
When parsed, `num_subgroups` is evaluated up to 255 while `CONFIG_BT_BAP_BASS_MAX_SUBGROUPS` (typically 1) only allocates 1 subgroup slot. Indices `i >= 1` index out of bounds and write arbitrary metadata into memory following `recv_state`.

In `samples/subsys/modbus/tcp_server/src/main.c`, `server_raw_cb` executes:
```c
tmp_adu.trans_id = adu->trans_id;
tmp_adu.proto_id = adu->proto_id;
tmp_adu.length = adu->length;
tmp_adu.unit_id = adu->unit_id;
tmp_adu.fc = adu->fc;
memcpy(tmp_adu.data, adu->data,
       MIN(adu->length, CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE));
```
`struct modbus_adu` is defined in `include/zephyr/modbus/modbus.h` as:
```c
struct modbus_adu {
    uint16_t trans_id;
    uint16_t proto_id;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t unit_id;
    uint8_t fc;
    uint8_t data[CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE - 4];
    uint16_t crc;
};
```
Notice that the destination buffer `tmp_adu.data` has a capacity of `CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE - 4` bytes. However, `memcpy` clamps the copy length using `MIN(adu->length, CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE)` instead of `sizeof(tmp_adu.data)` (or `CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE - 4`).

When an ADU with `adu->length > CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE - 4` is processed, `memcpy` copies up to `CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE` bytes into `tmp_adu.data`, overflowing the buffer by up to 4 bytes into the subsequent `crc` field and adjacent memory structures (such as `received` semaphore or global state).

**Conceptual Exploit Payload:**
An external attacker connects to the Modbus TCP server on port 502 and sends a Modbus TCP frame with an MBAP header specifying a data length field (bytes 4-5) of `CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE` (e.g., 256 or default buffer size) followed by payload data. When the server processes the raw response/ADU, `adu->length` exceeds `CONFIG_MODBUS_BUFFER_SIZE - 4`, triggering `memcpy()` to write beyond `tmp_adu.data` bounds.

