In `drivers/i2c/i2c_nrfx_twim.c`, the `i2c_nrfx_twim_transfer` function handles concatenated I2C read transfers into an internal driver buffer (`msg_buf`). After a successful transfer, it copies the received data back into each message's buffer in reverse order:

```c
186: 			int j = i;
187: 
188: 			while (msg_buf_used >= msgs[j].len) {
189: 				msg_buf_used -= msgs[j].len;
190: 				memcpy(msgs[j].buf,
191: 				       msg_buf + msg_buf_used,
192: 				       msgs[j].len);
193: 				j--;
194: 			}
```

The `while` loop completely lacks a bounds check `j >= 0`. 
When the loop processes the first message of the transaction (`j == 0`), `msg_buf_used` is decremented by `msgs[0].len`, reaching 0. Then `j--` decrements `j` to `-1`.
On the subsequent loop iteration check, the loop condition `msg_buf_used >= msgs[j].len` accesses `msgs[-1].len`, which is an out-of-bounds memory read (CWE-125).
Furthermore, if the memory preceding `msgs` has a value of 0 at the offset of `len` (or when `msg_buf_used` and `msgs[-1].len` are both 0), `0 >= 0` evaluates to true. The loop then executes with `j = -1`, performing `memcpy(msgs[-1].buf, msg_buf + msg_buf_used, msgs[-1].len)` and further decrementing `j`, leading to out-of-bounds writes and severe memory corruption.

